    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d377">

                <pb facs="0609" n="495" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg495"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a4 Personnel/16: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the British Secretary of
                        State for Foreign Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Henderson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-05-21">May 21, 1931</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>May I express to you the profound pleasure with which I have received the news of
                    your appointment as President of the forthcoming general disarmament
                        conference.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d377-f1"/> In view of your sincere and
                    enthusiastic leadership in the worldwide movement toward an effective limitation
                    and reduction of armaments, I believe that no wiser choice could have been made.
                    Please accept my sincere personal congratulations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry L. Sttmson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d377-f1" n="6" xml:id="d377-f1-content">
                <p>See League of Nations, <hi rendition="#italic">Official Journal</hi>, July 1981,
                    pp. 1113 and 1129.</p>
            </note>
